<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_rc-discharge" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Discharging a Capacitor</title>
	
		<activity>
      	<title>Warm-up</title>
	  	<introduction>
      			<p></p>
				<p>The circuit below contains a capacitor, an open switch, and a light bulb.  The capacitor has known capacitance <m>C</m>, charge <m>+Q_o</m> on the top plate, and charge <m>-Q_o</m> on the bottom plate, as shown in the figure.</p>
					
				<figure xml:id="RC0">
			  	<title>Capacitor Discharge Figure</title>
				<caption>A circuit diagram with both a light bulb and a capacitor.</caption>
				<image source="RC-d.png" width = "50%" ><description>A circuit diagram with a charged capacitor on the left (positive on top), an open switch, and a light bulb on the right.</description></image>
				</figure>
	  	</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use the definition of <xref ref="def_capacitance"/> to write an equation for the voltage across the capacitor.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					See the definition of the <xref ref="a_capacitance"/>.
				</p>
			</answer>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose the switch is closed at <m>t = 0</m>.  For an instant immediately after the switch is closed, sketch a voltage diagram for the circuit.  How does the voltage across the light bulb compare to the voltage across the capacitor?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Give a qualitative description of what you think will happen to the following two quantities as time moves forward: (1) the charge on the capacitor and (2) the brightness of the light bulb.  Support your answers with a mechanistic explanation.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sketch a graph predicting the <em>current</em> through the circuit as a function of time, starting at <m>t = 0</m>.  Comment on the value of the current in the limit as <m>t \rightarrow \infty</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		</activity>
					
		

		<!--		
