  <subsubsection><title>Derivation: Discharging Capacitor</title></subsubsection>

	<p>
		Consider now a circuit like the one above in which the light bulb is replaced with a <xref ref="a_resistor"/> with resistance <m>R</m>.  Such a circuit is often termed an <term>RC-circuit</term>.
	</p>

	<figure xml:id="RC0-1">
			  	<title>Capacitor Discharge Figure 2</title>
				<caption>A circuit diagram with both a resistor and a capacitor.</caption>
				<image source="RC-d2.png" width = "50%" ><description>A circuit diagram with a charged capacitor on the left (positive on top), an open switch, and a resistor on the right.</description></image>
	</figure>

	<p>
		You can use the <xref ref="loop-rule"/> to relate the voltages across the different circuit elements: 
		<me>V_C + V_R = 0</me>
		<me>\frac{Q(t)}{C} - I(t)R = 0</me>
		<me>\frac{Q(t)}{C} = I(t)R</me>
		You can now leverage that the current through the resistor <m>I(t)</m> is the rate of change at which charge <m>Q(t)</m> is <em>decreasing</em> on the positive plate, giving <m>I(t) = -\frac{dQ(t)}{dt}</m>
		<me>\frac{Q(t)}{C} = -R\frac{dQ(t)}{dt}</me>
		<me>\frac{dQ(t)}{dt} = -\frac{Q(t)}{RC}</me>
		You can solve this differential equation using separation of variables to find the charge on the capacitor as a function of time:
		<me>\frac{dQ}{Q} = -\frac{dt}{RC}</me>
		<me>\int_{Q_o}^{Q(t)} \frac{dQ}{Q} = -\int_0^t \frac{dt'}{RC}</me>
		<me>\ln{\frac{Q(t)}{Q_o}} = -\frac{t}{RC}</me>
		<me>Q(t) = Q_o e^{-t/RC}</me>
	</p>
	
		
  		<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		<activity>
      <title>Capacitor Behavior</title>
      <p></p>
			<p>Provide a physical argument to justify the generalized statement below about capacitors.</p>
			<assumption xml:id="a_capacitor-continuity">
		<title>Capacitor Continuity</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				The voltage across an ideal capacitor in a circuit should be continuous, but the current through an ideal capacitor does not have to be continuous.
			</p>
			
		</statement>
	</assumption>
		</activity>
		<activity>
      <title>Exploring RC Circuits</title>
	  <introduction>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			Consider a discharging capacitor for the following questions.
		</p>
	  </introduction>
	  <task>
		<statement>
			<p>Sketch a graph of the voltage across the discharging capacitor as a function of time.</p>
		</statement>
	  </task>
	  <task>
			<p>Determine the units of the quantity <m>RC</m>.  Describe how the graph you made would change if you were to <em>increase</em> this quantity.  Use your answers to give a brief physical interpretation for it (that is, what <em>kind</em> of quantity is it, and what is it trying to tell you about the behavior of the circuit?).</p>
	  </task>
		<task>
			<p>Calculate an expression for the current through the resistor as a function of time.</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>Sketch a graph of the current through the resistor as a function of time.  Make sense of this graph given your answers to the previous activities.</p>
		</task>
		</activity>
	</subsubsection>

<!--NO AI-->
</section>

